<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-lysine degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY0-461" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: TAX-562| is able to achieve low growth on |FRAME: LYS| as the sole source of nitrogen; however, unlike other bacteria, it is unable to utilize L-lysine as the sole source of carbon and energy. &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; appears to utilize this lysine degradation pathway as an adaptation to stationary phase growth conditions |CITS: [30498244]|. The pathway was experimentally validated by tracing &lt;sup&gt;13&lt;/sup&gt;C- and &lt;sup&gt;15&lt;/sup&gt;N-labeled L-lysine in growth experiments |CITS: [30498244]|.</Description>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
